ould be taught about  the emotional side of puberty as well as  the physical changes like the social  side and kind of how it sort of impacts  on your self-esteem everyone needs to be  taught about it not just our age or like  not just adults like even boys our age  needs to be taught about it so they  understand that it's a thing that we all  go through people should be more open  about these kind of things it's just  what happens to you it is a part of life  and it shouldn't be seen as something  you know that's inappropriate it's clear  not enough is being done to support us  as we get older we need to bust a myth  that teenage girls are dropping out just  because we're not interested I want to  see a future where we can talk openly  about puberty and never feel alone with  it I want to see a future where girls  can take part in sports without the fear  of being judged I want to see a future  where we can feel confident about how we  look no matter what changes we are going  through  thanks Isabel well we all know that  puberty is a really important subject  and there are loads of questions but  sometimes we're just too embarrassed to  ask so we called in an expert  fyi's Maya took up the challenge with  her questions for Dr Alex George the  government's youth Ambassador for mental  health  hi Dr Alex how are you I'm very well  thank you so for those of us who don't  know what is puberty and when does it  start puberty put simply as the change  as we go from being a child to being an  adult generally around eight years old  who's at the age of what 16 or 17 we go  through changes in our body and our  hormones change even our minds change as  well as we mature this is different  between boys and girls so what does it  mean for us girls you know what kind of  things happen to our bodies so in girls  breast develop we grow hair in different  places like for example under the  armpits and in the pubic regions and  also the the brain basically tells the  ovaries to st